\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: The game is in the initial position, with all pieces on their starting squares. White has the first-move advantage and aims to develop pieces actively while controlling the center. Black’s setup is symmetrical, so this is a classical opening scenario.\n\nEvaluation:  \n- Advancing central pawns (e.g., e2-e4 or d2-d4) is highly desirable to control the center (e4, d4, e5, d5). These moves open diagonals for the queen and bishops and are standard in most openings.\n- Knight development (e.g., N: g1-f3 or N: b1-c3) is also strong, as it prepares kingside castling and exerts pressure on central squares.\n- Moving edge pawns like a2-a3 or h2-h3 is less effective at this stage, as they don’t contribute to central control.\n- Jumping with knights to a3 or h3 is awkward because they don't influence the center effectively.\n\nTop candidates:\n- P: e2-e4 — Controls d5 and f5, opens lines for queen and bishop. Most popular opening move in chess history.\n- P: d2-d4 — Similar benefits, leads to Queen’s Pawn Game; also excellent.\n- N: g1-f3 — Develops knight to a strong square, eyes e5 and d4, supports future central play.\n\nReason: P: e2-e4 is the most principled and aggressive choice. It maximizes central control, accelerates development, and is statistically associated with high winning chances across all levels of play. It avoids premature commitment of other pieces while inviting dynamic play.\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4"}
