\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d3-c3, Q: d3-b3, Q: d3-a3, Q: d3-c4, Q: d3-b5, Q: d3-d2, Q: d3-d1, Q: d3-e2, Q: d3-e3, Q: d3-f3, Q: d3-g3, Q: d3-h3, Q: d3-e4, Q: d3-f5, Q: d3-g6, Q: d3-h7, P: d4-d5, K: e1-d1, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, B: f1-e2, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-e2, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in the early-mid game stage. My pawn on d4 is centrally advanced, supported by the queen on d3, exerting pressure in the center. Black has a queen on d8 and a bishop on f6, both potentially threatening, but many of Black’s pieces are not optimally developed. Notably, Black’s king appears to be still in the center (on f7?), which is highly unusual and possibly a vulnerability—kingside castling seems not to have occurred. My development is partially complete: knights and bishops are on their starting squares or have moved once, and my queen is already active. However, my king remains in the center, so caution is needed.\n\nBlack has a pawn on b5 (PBF1), suggesting it just moved two squares from b7 to b5, making it eligible for en passant capture. However, no white pawn is on c4 to exploit that immediately. The black king being on f7 is extremely exposed—this is a critical observation. It may be a blunder or intentional trap, but it presents tactical opportunities.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Qd3-h7: This move attacks h7, which is defended only by the black king. But h7 is also protected by the black rook on h8. Still, if the king is truly on f7, then Qxh7+ would deliver check, and Kh8 would lose the rook. This is a strong possibility.\n- Qd3-g6: Targets g6, eyeing the weak dark squares around the black king. Could prepare for a mating net.\n- Qd3-f5: Applies pressure on f5, possibly targeting f7 if supported.\n- Nf3-e5 or Ng1-f3: Already possible via N: g1-f3 in the valid moves list. Developing the knight increases control over central squares.\n- Pd4-d5: Gains space and restricts Black’s counterplay, but may overextend without full development.\n- Qd3-h7 is particularly tempting: if the black king is on f7, then Qxh7+ forces either Kf8 (exposing to material loss) or Kh8 (losing the rook). In either case, White gains a decisive advantage.\n\nWait — looking again at the board: Black king is listed as **KBF1** on **f7**? That is highly irregular. Kings start on e8. If the king is on f7, it must have moved there, possibly due to an earlier check or blunder. Regardless, its presence on f7 makes it extremely vulnerable.\n\nQd3-h7 delivers check only if h7 is adjacent to f7—which it is not. f7 to h7 is two files apart. So Qd3-h7 does *not* check the king on f7. However, Qd3-g6 comes close: g6 is adjacent to f7. So Qg6 would attack f7 directly—i.e., **check**.\n\nBut wait: Qd3-g6 is along the diagonal? d3 to g6 is a diagonal move (d3-e4-f5-g6), yes. And g6 is one square away from f7 (diagonally adjacent). So Qg6 does **not** attack f7. Only orthogonal or diagonal adjacent squares.\n\nWhat squares attack f7?\n\n- Queen or bishop on e6, g6, e8, g8, d5, etc.—but more importantly:\n- A queen on g6 attacks h7, f5, e4, etc., but **not f7**.\n- A queen on e5 attacks f7 diagonally (e5-f6-g7… no). e5 to f7 is a knight move, not queen line.\n\nActually, **only a piece on e6, g6, e8, g8, d7, h7, d5, h5** can attack f7 via straight lines.\n\nLet’s compute:\n\n- Diagonal attackers to f7: e6, g6, e8, g8\n- Orthogonal: f6, f8, d7, h7\n\nSo to attack f7, we need a piece on one of those.\n\nCurrently, do any white pieces threaten f7?\n\n- Our queen on d3 can go to e4, f5, g6, h7 — but none of these are attacking f7 directly.\n- But **Qd3-h7** attacks h7. If Black plays ...Kh8, it's safe. But if the king is on f7, it cannot move to h7 (not adjacent). f7 to g8 is legal, f7 to e7, f7 to e8, f7 to g7.\n\nWait — f7 is adjacent to:\n- e7, g7, e8, f8, g8\n\nSo h7 is **not** adjacent to f7. Therefore, **Qh7 does not check the king on f7**, nor attack it.\n\nThen how can we attack f7?\n\nOnly via:\n\n- Bc1-h6: Bh6 attacks g7 and f8, but not f7.\n- Bc1-g5: g5 — now, g5 to f6, e7, f4... not f7.\n- Bc1-e3: e3 to f4, g5, d4, c5 — no.\n- What about **Nf3**? From f3, knight goes to g5, e5, d4, d2, e1, g1 — none near f7.\n\nAlternatively, **Ne5**: from e5, knight attacks f7! Yes — **knight on e5 attacks f7**.\n\nSo a promising plan: play N: g1-f3, preparing Nf3-e5 to attack f7.\n\nBut is Ne5 possible? After Nf3, next move Ne5 only if f3-e5 is valid. But first, we must develop.\n\nAnother idea: **Bc1-h6** — Bh6 attacks g7. If g7 is defended only by the king, then Bxg7 wins material, as king must recapture, exposing it.\n\nLooking at g7: currently occupied by PBF0 — a black pawn. So Bh6 x g7 would be a trade: bishop for pawn. But after Bxg7, Kxg7, the black king is still on g7 — very exposed.\n\nThen Qd3-g6+ would check the king on g7! Because g6 is adjacent to g7.\n\nYes! So:\n\n1. Bc1-h6: attacks g7\n2. If Pxg7? No, black pawn is on g7 — but if they don’t capture, Bxg7 forces Kxg7\n3. Then Qd3-g6+! — check on g7\n   - King cannot go to h8 (attacked by queen)\n   - Only escape: Kg8 or Kh7\n   - But h7 is undefended? Rook on h8 — but queen attacks h7\n   - So Kh7?? Qxh7#!\n   - Kg8 — then Qxg7#? No: Qg6 to g7 is not checkmate — king on g8, queen on g7 — yes, that’s mate if no defenders.\n\nAfter Bxg7, Kxg7, Qg6+:\n\n- Kg8 → Qxg7#? No: queen on g6, capturing on g7 would leave g7, but g7 is empty. Qg6 to g7 is not check — it's same file, but g7 is behind.\n\nWait: Qg6+ checks on g6. After Kg8, Qg6 still attacks g8? Yes — vertically. So Qg6 gives check on g8. So if king goes to g8, **Qg6#** — because g8 is attacked, and no escape.\n\nKing on g7, checked by queen on g6:\n\n- Can go to g8 → Qg6 still attacks g8? Yes — same file. So Qg6 gives check on both g7 and g8.\n- So if king moves from g7 to g8, Qg6 still checks — and no flight squares (h8 is attacked by queen via diagonal? Qg6 to h7 — yes, h7 is attacked. h8 is one step further — not directly attacked unless by other pieces).\n\nQueen on g6 attacks:\n\n- Rank 6: all of rank 6\n- File g: all of g-file\n- Diagonals: f5, e4, d3, c2, b1 and h5, h7, h8? g6 to h7 is one diagonal square — yes. h8 is beyond — yes, if unblocked.\n\nFrom g6 to h7: yes. h7 to h8: blocked by nothing? h7 is empty? Currently h7 has PBF0 — pawn. So h7 is occupied, so queen on g6 does **not** attack h8.\n\nSo after Kxg7, Qg6+:\n\n- King on g7, checked by queen on g6 (same file)\n- Legal moves: f7, f8, g8, h7, h8?\n  - f7: possible? f7 has no piece — yes\n  - f8: empty — yes\n  - g8: empty — yes\n  - h7: has black pawn — no\n  - h8: rook — no\n- So king escapes to f7, f8, or g8\n\nNot mate.\n\nBut still, we win a pawn and expose the king.\n\nBetter idea: **Qd3-h7** — even if not checking f7, it threatens Rh8. If Black doesn't respond, Qxh8 wins the rook.\n\nBut Black can simply play ...Kf8 or ...Kg8, escaping danger.\n\nBut wait — what if we play **Qd3-h7** and Black ignores it? Then Qxh8, Rxh8, but then our queen is gone, and Black’s king is still exposed.\n\nAlternatively, **Qd3-f5** — eyes f7? f5 to f7 is two ranks up — not attacking. But f5 to g6, e6, etc.\n\nNo direct attack.\n\nBest immediate tactical shot: **Bc1-h6**\n\nIf Black allows Bxg7, then Kxg7, Qg6+ forces the king into the open.\n\nEven better: after Bxg7 Kxg7, Qg6+ Kg8, then Qxg7 is not forced, but we can play Nh5!? or something later.\n\nBut let's see: is Bh6 in the valid moves?\n\nValid moves include: **B: c1-h6**\n\nYes!\n\nAlso, **Q: d3-h7**, **Q: d3-g6**, etc.\n\nAnother candidate: **Qd3-h7** — threatens Qxh8, winning a rook for a queen? No, equal material, but if it leads to mate, good.\n\nBut Bh6 seems more forcing and dangerous.\n\nAlternatively, **N: g1-f3** — solid development, supports e5 push later.\n\nBut given the exposed black king, we should prioritize aggression.\n\nBest move: **B: c1-h6**\n\nReason: It initiates a powerful sacrificial attack. By threatening Bxg7, it forces the black king to make weakening moves. If Black captures (unlikely), we recapture and maintain pressure. If not, we play Bxg7, Kxg7, Qg6+ forces the king into the open, where it will be mated or lose massive material. This aggressive approach capitalizes on Black’s king position on f7 — likely a fatal error.\n\nAnswer: B: c1-h6"}
