**Qh5+!!** becomes a deadly threat.\n\nCan I play Qh5+ after dxe5? Yes — next move.\n\nSequence:\n1. dxe5 (removes e5 pawn)\n   - Now, Black has no pawn on e5, king on f7\n2. Next, if not stopped, Qd1-d3 and Qh5+ is coming\n   - Qh5+ forces Ke6 or Kg8\n   - If Ke6, then f4-f5+? or Bc4+ etc.\n\nBut even better: **Right after dxe5, is Qh5+ possible? No — queen still on d1. But preparing it is powerful.**\n\nMoreover, **B: f1-c4** supports Qh5+ because if Black plays g6 to block, Bxg6 hxg6 Qxg6+ is a motif.\n\nBut back to **P: d4-e5** — it enables immediate central control and mating threats.\n\nAnother benefit: prevents Black from using e5 for counterplay.\n\nAlso, it’s a free pawn gain — en passant wins a pawn.\n\nSo **P: d4-e5** is strong: gains material, opens lines, accelerates attack.\n\nCompare to **B: f1-c4** — also good, but doesn’t win a pawn.\n\nThus, best move: **P: d4-e5**\n\nReason: It captures en passant, gaining a pawn and opening the e-file. It exploits Black’s premature e5 advance while their king is stranded on f7. It enables rapid development with attacking chances like Qh5+, Bc4, and Nf3, threatening quick mate.\n\nAnswer: P: d4-e5'}
