s a knight, so Kd7 is likely forced).\n\nAfter Kd7, I can follow up with e6-e7, preparing e8=Q with crushing effect.\n\nAlternatively, if I don’t act fast, Black might develop counterplay with ...Nc6 or ...Qe7.\n\nBut **Q: h7-h8+** is **immediate check**, forcing the king into the center, disrupting coordination, and accelerating my attack.\n\nMoreover, it’s one of the few forcing moves in the position.\n\nOther moves like P:e7 or B:e2 are slow in comparison.\n\nReason: Delivering check maximizes pressure, limits Black’s responses, and accelerates the attack on the exposed Black king. The queen move to h8 is both aggressive and tactically sound, with no risk.\n\nAnswer: Q: h7-h8"}
