–c6–d7–e8 — does **not** control d5.\n          - f5: same — not on diagonal.\n          - d6: not attacked.\n          - f6: not attacked.\n          - e4: possible?\n          - But wait — **Ne6+ gives check**, and there is **no capture** possible, and **no blocking** (knight can't be blocked). So king **must move**.\n          - Legal king moves: d5, d6, e6 (no — occupied), f5, f6, d4? Too far. One square only.\n          - From e5: d5, d6, e6, f6, f5, f4, e4, d4 — all within one square.\n          - But only d5, d6, f5, f6, e4, d4, f4 are candidates.\n          - None of these are currently attacked by other white pieces except possibly after follow-up.\n          - But **after Ne6+**, regardless of where king goes, I will have gained tempo and initiative, possibly leading to rapid attack.\n\nBut wait — is e6 attacked by black? Can black recapture if I land? No — knight on e6 cannot be captured unless black has piece on d4, f4, c5, c7, etc. — none present. So **Ne6+ is a free check**, and very powerful.\n\nCompare to N: g5-e4 — less aggressive, not a check.\n\nAlso consider: **B: b5-d3** — targets e4 and could support central play, but again, not as urgent.\n\nAnother candidate: **P: a2-a4** or others — too slow.\n\nBest move is clearly **N: g5-e6**, delivering check and crashing into black’s position.\n\nReason: The black king is wide open on e5, and Nxe6+ delivers a devastating check that cannot be blocked or captured, forcing the king into an even worse position, likely losing material or leading to immediate mating attacks. This maximizes winning chances by exploiting the most serious positional imbalance: king safety.\n\nAnswer: N: g5-e6"}
