like position.\n\nKing on g7 is very exposed — especially to attacks from rook on h6.\n\nNow, **R: h6-h7** would attack the black king directly — is that check?\n\nFrom h6 to h7 — same file, up one square. Black king is on g7 — adjacent, but not on h7.\n\nSo Rh7 does NOT give check — it just occupies h7.\n\nBut if I play **R: h6-h8**, then rook goes to h8 — now, is that checking the king on g7?\n\nDistance: h8 to g7 is diagonal — one square diagonally — so yes, that’s adjacent. So Rook on h8 attacks g7 — yes! It gives check.\n\nBecause rook moves horizontally/vertically — from h8, it attacks entire 8th rank and h-file.\n\ng7 is on the same rank? No — g7 is rank 7. h8 is rank 8.\n\nSo rook on h8 attacks all squares on rank 8 and file h.\n\ng7 is file g, rank 7 — not on same file or rank — so **rook on h8 does NOT attack g7**.\n\nAh! Critical mistake.\n\nRook only attacks vertically and horizontally.\n\nSo from h8, it attacks:\n\n- All of file h: h1 to h8\n- All of rank 8: a8 to h8\n\nBut g7 is not on file h nor rank 8 — it\'s one square diagonally away — so **not attacked by rook on h8**.\n\nTherefore, **R: h6-h8** is safe but not giving check — it just occupies the back rank.\n\nHowever, is there any danger? Could Black capture it? Currently, no black piece attacks h8 — so it\'s safe.\n\nBut better use might be **R: h6-h7** — because h7 is on the same rank as the black king (rank 7), file h.\n\nFrom h7, rook attacks entire rank 7 — including g7 (black king)! So **Rh7+** would be check!\n\nYes!\n\nCurrently, rook is on h6 — moving to h7 is one square up — valid vertical move.\n\nAfter Rh7+, black king on g7 is in check from rook on h7.\n\nCan black escape? Let\'s see:\n\n- King can go to h8? Yes — if not attacked.\n- King can go to f7? But f7 has a white knight (NWF1) — occupied by friendly? No — f7 has NWF1 — white knight — so enemy piece, cannot move there.\n- King can go to h7? But that’s where rook is — occupied.\n- King can go to f8, g8, h8?\n\nAvailable squares