m d8 to f7 — is that a check on e7?\n\nDistance: f7 to e7 is one file left — adjacent. Knight doesn’t attack adjacent squares. So no.\n\nSo **not a check**.\n\nBut after **R: g8-g7**, it *is* a check.\n\nAlso, consider **R: g8-h8**, **g8-f8**, etc. — less aggressive.\n\nOther rook moves: **R: d2-d1**, **d3**, etc. — but d-file is closed, and no immediate target.\n\nPawn moves: a2-a4, b2-b4 — flank pushes, slow.\n\nBut **R: g8-g7** delivers check and activates a rook.\n\nIt also may lead to quick attack: after king moves, I might follow with Q or other pieces — but I don’t have a queen visible. Possibly captured.\n\nBut still, activating the rook with tempo is powerful.\n\nIs the rook on g8 really mine? RWF1 — White rook, never moved. Yes.\n\nCould it be trapped? After g8-g7, no — it\'s moving to open file.\n\nAlso, is there any risk? For example, if Black plays ...Kxg7, but that’s not possible now — king is on e7.\n\nAfter Rg7+, suppose Black plays Ke6, then I can consider Nf7 or other threats.\n\nBut most importantly: **R: g8-g7** is a forcing move (check), gains tempo, and centralizes the rook.\n\nNo other move on the list gives such immediate pressure.\n\nCompare to **N: d8-e6** — attacks f8, g7, etc., but not check.\n\nOr **N: d8-f7** — occupies f7, which is near king, but again, not a threat.\n\nWhereas **R: g8-g7** is direct.\n\nTherefore, best move is **R: g8-g7**.\n\nAnswer: R: g8-g7'}
