\n1 |    |    |KWF1|BBF1|    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, K: c1-b1, K: c1-d1, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, R: d2-d1, R: d2-d3, R: d2-d4, R: d2-d5, R: d2-e2, N: d8-b7, N: d8-c6, N: d8-e6, N: d8-f7, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, R: g7-f7, R: g7-e7, R: g7-d7, R: g7-c7, R: g7-b7, R: g7-a7, R: g7-g6, R: g7-g5, R: g7-g4, R: g7-g3, R: g7-g2, R: g7-g1, R: g7-g8, R: g7-h7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king (KWF1) is on c1, having already moved, so castling is not possible. The black knight on d8 is active, and Black has a rook (RWF1) on g7 — which is actually a white piece mistakenly labeled (should be BW or BR if black; but based on position and context, likely RWF1 is an error, but we proceed with given labels). However, per the encoding, RWF1 on g7 would be a **White** rook, which makes sense as it's on my side of initiative. Wait — correction: g7 has **RWF1**, which is a **White** rook — yes, that’s mine, advanced to the 7th rank. That’s a strong asset. Black has pawns on a7, c5, d5, and a knight on d8. My pawn structure is mostly intact on the second rank, except c2 and f2.\n\nCritical observations:\n- My rook on g7 is deep in enemy territory but potentially vulnerable to counterattack.\n- Black’s king is on f8 (KBF1), also already moved.\n- No immediate threats to my king.\n- Central control is weak; d4/e4 are unoccupied.\n- I have undeveloped pieces, especially queenside.\n- The rook on g7 can retreat or push forward (e.g., g8 for potential check).\n\nPromising moves:\n1. **R: g7-g8** — Threatens check (if king is on f8), possibly forcing Kf7 or capturing, leading to material gain or checkmate prep. Since KBF1 is on f8, Rg8+ would be check. This is aggressive and tactically strong.\n2. **P: c2-c4** — Gains space in the center, supports d4 push later, activates the game.\n3. **N: d8-b7?** — Wait, no: N: d8-x moves are for Black knight. But in valid moves list, they are listed as *my* options — contradiction. Looking back: “Your valid moves are” includes **N: d8-b7**, etc. That implies *I* control the black knight? Impossible.\n\nAh — here’s the confusion: the move list must reflect *my* (White’s) legal moves only. Therefore, **N: d8-b7** cannot be a valid move for White unless I control that knight. But d8 has **NWF1** — a *White* knight on d8? That contradicts the starting position (knights start on b1/g1). But per the board: d8 has NWF1 → White knight on d8. So somehow, a White knight is on d8 — perhaps from promotion or prior move.\n\nGiven the state, we accept the board as is: **NWF1 on d8** — White knight on d8. So yes, I can move it.\n\nSo reevaluating:\n\n- Knight on d8 (NWF1): can go to b7, c6, e6, f7 — all plausible.\n  - **N: d8-f7** attacks g5 or e5, but more importantly, f7 is near Black’s king.\n  - **N: d8-c6** centralizes.\n  - **N: d8-f7** could fork ideas if supported.\n\nBut strongest idea:\n- **R: g7-g8+** — delivers check! Because black king is on f8. Rg8+ forces either Kf7 (king walks into open) or Kxg8 Qxg8, but Black has no queen mentioned — but even so, if Kxg8, I can follow up with other attacks.\n\nWait: if Rg7 to g8, is it safe? Can Black capture it? Currently, no Black piece attacks g8. Black has pawns on a7, c5, d5; knight on d8 (but it's White’s own knight — wait no: d8 has NWF1 — White knight. So Black has no knight. Black pieces visible: KBF1 (f8), PBF0 (a7), PBF1 (c5, d5), and... that’s it? And RWF1 is White’s rook on g7.\n\nSo Black is missing many pieces — likely this is endgame or midgame after heavy exchanges.\n\nThus, **R: g7-g8+** is very strong — check with rook, and Black must respond:\n\n- Kf7 — then Rh8 or Nxg8 or other follow-up\n- Or Kxg8 — then I have a rook trade, but since I’m White and likely ahead in material, this may lead to winning endgame.\n\nBut let’s compare:\n- **R: g7-g8** is in the valid move list — yes.\n- Also, **R: g7-h7** — secures h-file, but less forcing.\n\nOther candidate: **N: d8-f7** — attacks nothing immediately, but could threaten mate if supported.\n\nBut **Rg8+** is immediate and forcing — best practice is to make checks and captures when they gain tempo.\n\nAlso, after Rg8+, if Kxg8, then I recapture with knight: Nxf7, regaining rook and forking toward win.\n\nSo Rg8+ leads to either:\n- Material gain (rook for nothing if king runs)\n- Or clean exchange into winning endgame\n\nBest move: **R: g7-g8**\n\nAnswer: R: g7-g8"}
