\nCons: king still undeveloped.\n\nBut now, after rook captures, I can later play Kc1-c2 anyway.\n\nBut is there a difference?\n\nWait — if I play **R: d2-c2**, then Black can respond... but with what?\n\nBlack has no immediate threat.\n\nBut consider: after **R: d2-c2**, Black might play ...Kf7-e6 or something.\n\nBut I’m up a piece — winning.\n\nSimilarly, if I play **K: c1-c2**, same result — up a piece.\n\nBut **king capture vs rook capture** — which is better?\n\nThe rook is more valuable than the king for activity — but here, using the rook to capture keeps the king safer and develops the rook.\n\nBut actually, capturing with the rook is better because:\n\n- The rook is already developed and can be used for other things.\n- The king doesn’t need to rush into the center yet — though it eventually should.\n\nBut more importantly: after **K: c1-c2**, the king is on c2 — which is fine, but then my rook on d2 is still behind.\n\nBut no real danger.\n\nHowever, there’s a subtle point: **en passant or future pawn breaks**?\n\nNot really.\n\nAnother thing: **after capturing with rook, I open the c-file for the rook**.\n\nBut if I capture with king, then rook stays on d2.\n\nBut either way, I gain the piece.\n\nBut let’s see — is **K: c1-c2** even legal?\n\nc2 has an enemy piece — yes, Black bishop — so king can capture it.\n\nAnd king is not in check — because bishop on c2 is on same file? No — c1 and c2 are same file — vertical.\n\nBut king and bishop are on same file — but bishop moves diagonally, not vertically.\n\nSo the Black bishop does **not** attack c1 — because it’s on c2, directly above — but bishops don’t move straight.\n\nSo the king is **not in check**.\n\nTherefore, **K: c1-c2** is legal.\n\nSimilarly, **R: d2-c2** is a horizontal move — d2 to c2 is left one square — yes, rank 2.\n\nSo both are valid.\n\nNow, which one to choose?\n\nCapturing with the **rook** is better because:\n\n- The rook is a long-range piece — better suited for capturing and controlling files.\n- The king doesn’t need to take risks early, even if safe.\n- After rook captures, I can later bring the king out more safely.\n\nMoreover, after **R: d2-c2**, my rook is active on c2, eyeing the a-file or supporting b-pawn push.\n\nWhereas if I play **K: c1-c2**, I block potential b2-b4 push? No — king on c2 doesn’t block pawns.\n\nBut no obstruction.\n\nHowever, **principle**: use minor pieces or rooks to capture when possible, keep king safe.\n\nBut here, both are safe.\n\nBut there’s another consideration: **tempo**.\n\nIf I play **R: d2-c2**, I develop a piece and gain material.\n\nIf I play **K: c1-c2**, I develop the king and gain material — but king development is less urgent.\n\nMoreover, the rook was already on d2 — so it’s natural for it to make the capture.\n\nAdditionally, looking at the rook on e8 — it's already on the 8th rank, but not doing much.\n\nBut I can’t connect rooks easily.\n\nBut capturing the bishop is the priority.\n\nNow, are there any other strong moves?\n\nFor example, pushing pawns: a2-a4 or f2-f4 — to gain space.\n\nBut why do that when I can win a piece immediately?\n\nThat would be foolish.\n\nSimilarly, moving rook from e8 — but to where? Without coordination, it may not help.\n\nSo the best move is clearly one that captures the undefended bishop.\n\nBetween **R: d2-c2** and **K: c1-c2**, both win the bishop.\n\nBut **R: d2-c2** is better because:\n\n- It uses a piece of appropriate value.\n- Keeps the king safer.\n- Develops the rook actively.\n\nAlso, after Kc1-c2, the king is on c2 — which is okay, but then if I want to play b2-b4, the king might be in the way of future maneuvers? Not really.\n\nBut no downside to rook capture.\n\nIn fact, in endgames, rooks belong on open files — c-file might become open.\n\nAfter capturing on c2, rook controls the c-file.\n\nWhereas if king takes, rook stays on d2.\n\nSo **R: d2-c2** is superior.\n\nEvaluation:\n\n- R: d2-c2 — wins a bishop for free, activates rook, safe, improves position.\n- K: c1-c2 — also wins bishop, but less efficient use of king.\n- Pawn pushes — slow, don’t capitalize on opponent’s blunder.\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended Black bishop with the rook gains immediate material advantage without risk, while developing a major piece to an active square.\n\nAnswer: R: d2-c2"}
