\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a5, K: c4-b3, K: c4-b4, K: c4-b5, K: c4-c5, K: c4-d3, K: c4-d4, P: f4-f5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. The only pieces remaining are three pawns on a4 (White), b4 (Black), f4 (White), my king on c4 (KWF1), and the Black king on e6 (KBF1). The Black king is centralized but far from mine. My king is active and close to supporting pawn advances. There is no immediate threat of checkmate or capture.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a4-a5 – Pushing the a-pawn gains space but is likely futile; it's far from promotion and may become a weakness.\n- K: c4-b3 – Moving the king closer to the a4 pawn, potentially supporting its advance. However, this moves away from the center and key action.\n- K: c4-b4 – This move attacks the Black pawn on b4 directly. Capturing it next turn would be possible after this.\n- K: c4-b5 – This bypasses b4 and heads toward the queenside, but doesn't immediately challenge anything.\n- K: c4-c5 – Advances the king into more central control, possibly targeting d6 or e6 later.\n- K: c4-d3 – Retreats slightly, not productive.\n- K: c4-d4 – Centralizes the king further, keeps options open, supports f-pawn.\n- P: f4-f5 – Pushes the f-pawn, aiming for rapid advancement toward f7 and then f8 for promotion.\n\nThe most promising plans are either attacking the enemy pawn with K-b4 or advancing the f-pawn for a quick promotion race. The f-pawn is only 4 steps from queening (f4→f5→f6→f7→f8), while the Black king is far away on e6 and cannot easily stop it. My king can also support it if needed. In contrast, the a-pawn is too slow.\n\nReason: The fastest way to win is to promote a pawn. The f-pawn is on f4 and can advance quickly with minimal opposition. Pushing f4-f5 starts that initiative immediately. It gains space, maintains momentum, and threatens rapid advancement. Unlike king moves, which are slow and positional, this creates a concrete threat. The a4 pawn is less urgent.\n\nAnswer: P: f4-f5"}
