wn risks losing tempo. Since pawns cannot move backward, their advancement is more critical.\n\nThe most promising plan is to push the a-pawn. In endgames with few pawns, an advanced rook pawn (a-file) can be very effective because it pulls the enemy king far from the center, allowing the other side to gain space elsewhere. After a4-a5, the a-pawn threatens a6 and a7, forcing Black’s king to respond.\n\nReason: Advancing the a-pawn (a4-a5) creates a long-term threat of promotion that Black must address. It starts creating a passed pawn on the flank, potentially drawing Black’s king away from the center and giving White time to activate the king or push the f-pawn later. Compared to f5-f6, which is more easily stopped, a4-a5 offers better strategic winning chances.\n\nAnswer: P: a4-a5"}
