s critical tempo. After f6, even if Black plays K-e5-f6, my king can respond K-c4-d5 or similar to pressure. But more importantly, f5-f6 prevents Black from comfortably approaching — after f6, the pawn will queen next move unless captured, and Black cannot capture en passant (no double-step by enemy). This is a powerful forcing move.\n\nReason: The most urgent and winning move is to push the f-pawn to f6. It creates an immediate threat of queening on f7 next move, forcing the black king to react. Since Black has no pieces other than the king, delaying this advance risks allowing Black to capture the pawn. Pushing f5-f6 now maximizes pressure and minimizes counterplay.\n\nAnswer: P: f5-f6"}
