\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a6-a7, K: c4-b3, K: c4-b4, K: c4-b5, K: c4-c5, K: c4-d3, K: c4-d4, K: c4-d5, P: f6-f7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on c4, and I have two pawns: one on a6 and one on f6. The black king is on g8, with no other pieces remaining. Black has no immediate threats. My two pawns are advanced, and both are one step away from promotion (a6→a7 and f6→f7). The white king is active and central, but not immediately needed for pawn support due to the distance of the black king.\n\nEvaluation: \n- P: a6-a7 – Advances the a-pawn to the 7th rank. After this, it will be one square away from queening. However, the f-pawn is closer to promotion with less obstruction.\n- P: f6-f7 – Moves the f-pawn to f7, one square from queening. This is more central and faster to promote, especially since after f7-f8=Q, I can deliver check or control key squares near the black king.\n- King moves (c4-b3, b4, b5, c5, d3, d4, d5) – These help centralize the king or support pawns, but given that the black king is far away and not threatening anything, advancing a pawn toward promotion is more urgent and decisive.\n\nThe key insight is that promoting either pawn wins the game, but the f-pawn promotes faster and in a more dominant position. After f6-f7, next move f7-f8=Q cannot be stopped, and the new queen can immediately attack the black king or assist in mating. The a-pawn is on the edge and takes longer to leverage after promotion.\n\nReason: Promoting the f-pawn is faster and leads to a quicker checkmate. It is also safer, as the promoted queen will cover central escape squares for the black king. Therefore, f6-f7 is the most forcing and winning move.\n\nAnswer: P: f6-f7'}
