ey escape squares.\n- Q: g7-f7 places the queen on f7, attacking e5 and cutting off retreat to the 7th rank. It also avoids immediate loss and supports the approach of the white king.\n- K-moves like b5-a5 or b5-c5 bring my king closer to assist in mating. Centralizing the king (e.g., b5-c5) improves its influence.\n\nBest plan: Use the queen to restrict the black king’s mobility while approaching with the king to deliver checkmate. A strong first step is to place the queen on a square that controls multiple escape routes.\n\nQ: g7-f7 is strong because:\n- It attacks e5 (pinning the black king temporarily if he doesn’t move)\n- It prevents the black king from advancing up the board\n- It prepares for checks along the 7th rank or from the side\n- It does not allow capture (black king cannot take because it\'s defended by the white king? No—wait: distance between b5 and f7 is too far—the queen on f7 would be undefended against Kxe5? Wait: Black king is on e5. If queen goes to f7, then it attacks e5? Yes — f7 to e5 is diagonal (f7-e6-d5-c4… no, wait: f7 to e5 is one diagonal step: yes, f7-e6-d5-c4-b3-a2? No — actually, f7 to e5 is one square diagonally down-left: yes, that’s correct. So f7 *does* attack e5.\n\nWait: e5 to f7: difference is +1 file, +2 ranks — not diagonal. Diagonals change file and rank by equal amounts. So f7 to e5 is Δfile = -1, Δrank = -2 — not diagonal. Therefore, **the queen on f7 does NOT attack e5**.\n\nCorrect lines:\n- Queen on f7 attacks:\n  - Along rank 7: a7-h7\n  - Along file f: f1-f8\n  - Diagonals: from f7, diagonals are e8, d9… and g6, h5, etc. and e6, d5, c4, b3, a2; and g8.\n\nSo queen on f7 attacks h5? Let\'s see: f7 → g6 → h5: yes, that\'s diagonal (+1,+1 each step). But e5? From f7 to e6 is diagonal (-1,-1), then e6 to e5 is straight down — not diagonal. So f7 does not attack e5.\n\nDistance: f7 to e5 is |f-e|=1, |7-5|=2 — knight move, not queen line. So queen on f7 does NOT attack e5.\n\nThus, Q: g7-f7 does not check the king on e