roach the Black king diagonally**, cutting off its movement. The most effective way is to advance toward it using diagonal steps. Among the options, **K: e7-d8** or **K: e7-d7** may help triangulate, but the best square is **d6**—a strong central square that threatens to approach the black king.\n\nHowever, note that Black is on g7. The closest White can get is via d6, which is three files away. We need to **centralize and advance**.\n\nLet’s assess distance:\n- From e7 to f6 (not available) would be good, but not allowed.\n- Instead, **K: e7-d7** moves horizontally, not helpful.\n- **K: e7-d6** advances forward-left, gaining ground toward the center and reducing Black’s space.\n\nWait — **e7-d6** is a backward-left move, but d6 is closer to central control and allows future moves like c7 or e6.\n\nBut actually, the best move is **K: e7-e6**? No — that retreats.\n\nWait — all moves seem passive. But in king endings, **the player who has the move sometimes loses the opposition**.\n\nCurrent position: White Ke7, Black Kg7 — both on 7th rank, 2 files apart. It's **White to move**, so White cannot maintain the distant opposition — because being on the same rank with an even number of files between means the side to move loses the opposition.\n\nSo White must avoid letting Black seize the center. Therefore, White should **move to e8**, vacating the 7th rank so that after Black moves, White can take the opposition on the 7th rank.\n\nFor example:\n1. Ke8! Kf7 2. Kd7 — now White seizes the 7th rank with opposition.\nOr 1... Kh7 2. Kd7, centralizing.\n\nThus, **K: e7-e8** is a waiting move, transferring the burden to Black.\n\nReason: Moving the king to e8 gives up the current rank but allows White to regain the opposition next move depending on Black’s reply. It is a **waiting maneuver** to force Black into a less flexible position. This is a standard technique in king endgames when you don’t want to spoil your position by advancing prematurely.\n\nAnswer: K: e7-e8"}
