\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: g7-f7, K: g7-f8, K: g7-g8, K: g7-h6, K: g7-h7, K: g7-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: The board is nearly empty, with only two kings remaining. I am playing as White, and my king is on g7. The Black king is on f5. It is White's turn to move. The goal is to checkmate the Black king or force a stalemate, but since there are no pawns or other pieces besides the kings, checkmate is impossible. However, I must avoid stalemate (which would result in a draw) while trying to achieve a winning condition — but in this position, with only two kings, **checkmate is impossible**, so the game will end in a draw unless one side blunders into stalemate.\n\nCurrently, the Black king is on f5, and my (White's) king is on g7. The Black king has several legal moves, and it is not currently in check. My task is to make a legal move with the White king that does not immediately stalemate Black.\n\nEvaluation:\n- K: g7-f7 — Moves king to f7. This places the White king adjacent to the Black king (f5 and f7 are two squares apart vertically). This does not attack the Black king and leaves Black with full mobility (e5, e6, f6, g5, g6, etc.). Safe.\n- K: g7-f8 — Moves king to f8. Distant from Black king. No risk of stalemate. Black still has all moves.\n- K: g7-g8 — Moves king to g8. Also distant. Fine.\n- K: g7-h6 — Moves king to h6. Adjacent diagonally to g7, still far from f5. Black king retains mobility.\n- K: g7-h7 — To h7. Same.\n- K: g7-h8 — To h8. Far away.\n\nAll moves are essentially equivalent in outcome — a draw — because **king vs king endgames are drawn** due to insufficient mating material. There is no way for either side to deliver checkmate.\n\nHowever, the key is to **avoid stalemate**. If I play a move that leaves the Black king with no legal moves, it would be stalemate and the game is drawn — but technically a failure to win.\n\nBut in this case, none of the available moves cause stalemate. The Black king on f5 currently has access to e4, e5, e6, f6, g6, g5, g4 — many moves. Even after any of these king moves, Black will still have moves.\n\nTherefore, all moves are safe and equal in evaluation.\n\nReason: Since no checkmate is possible and all moves lead to a drawn endgame, any legal move is acceptable. I will choose a centralizing move to keep options open, though it matters little.\n\nAnswer: K: g7-f7"}
