ess due to impossibility of checkmate.\n\nBut again, **only kings remain** → **insufficient material for checkmate** → game is already a draw.\n\nStill, to maintain optimal form in a king vs king scenario (e.g., if more pieces were added later), we should minimize distance to the opponent’s king.\n\nCurrent distance (f7 to g4): \n- Rank difference: 3 (7–4)\n- File difference: 1 (f–g = 6–7 = -1)\n- Chebyshev distance = max(3,1) = 3\n\nBest approach: reduce distance.\n\nCandidate moves:\n- K: f7-e6 → e6 to g4: file diff 2, rank diff 2 → distance 2\n- K: f7-g6 → g6 to g4: same file, rank diff 2 → distance 2\n- K: f7-f6 → f6 to g4: file diff 1, rank diff 2 → distance 2\n- K: f7-g7 → g7 to g4: file same, rank diff 3 → distance 3 (no improvement)\n\nSo e6, f6, and g6 all improve proximity.\n\nBest among them? g6 puts the king on the same file as Black’s king, preparing potential opposition if Black advances. But more importantly, **g6 cuts off retreat** along the g-file.\n\nAlternatively, e6 heads toward the center and could support future flanking, but less direct.\n\nSince both sides have no pawns, and no possibility of promotion, the move is symbolic.\n\nBut per engine logic, **minimize distance + prepare for hypothetical mate**.\n\nIn king and pawn endgames, taking the distant opposition matters — but here, no pawns.\n\nThus, any approach is equivalent.\n\nBut let\'s pick **K: f7-g6**, reducing vertical distance and aligning on adjacent file.\n\nWait — g6 is on same file as Black king (g4), only two ranks apart. That gives White *opposition* if needed.\n\nBut better: **K: f7-f6** keeps flexibility.\n\nActually, **K: f7-e6** starts centralizing and approaching via diagonal — often key in king endgames.\n\nBut again, no real consequence.\n\nHowever, **K: f7-g6** directly pressures the g-file and reduces escape routes if Black ever advances.\n\nBut Black king is on g4 — moving to g6 would place White king on g6, one square diagonally adjacent to h5 and f5, but not yet threatening.\n\nMost principled: **approach with king using shortest path**.\n\nFrom f7 to g4: requires closing both file and rank gap.\n\nOptimal approach: move diagonally toward target.\n\nf7 → g6 → h5 → g4? No, that’s longer.\n\nf7 → g6 (closer), then g6 to g5 is next.\n\nBut f7 → e6 → d5 → c4 etc. drifts away.\n\nWait: f7 to g4: ideal path is f7 → g6 → g5 → g4? But that’s not forced.\n\nThe minimal move to reduce Chebyshev distance is best.\n\nAll of e6, f6, g6 reduce distance from 3 to 2.\n\nAmong them, **g6** places the king on the same file as the enemy king, which could simulate "shadowing" or preparation for box method.\n\nBut **e6** heads toward center.\n\nIn standard king vs king endgames, centralization helps.\n\nBut here, the black king is on g4 — slightly off-center.\n\nClosest central squares are e4, e5, f5, f4.\n\nSo moving to e6 doesn’t help much.\n\n**f6** is on same file as original position, but closer.\n\nBut **g6** is on same file as Black king — potentially useful for cutting off.\n\nIn Tarrasch rule style, cut off the king by occupying files.\n\nBut with only kings, irrelevant.\n\nNonetheless, **K: f7-g6** is a natural approach move.\n\nBut wait — is g6 safe?\n\nNo risk of self-stalemate since Black has room: g4 can go to f3, f4, f5, g5, h5, h4, h3 — plenty of moves.\n\nSo no danger.\n\nAlternatively, **K: f7-e7** moves sideways — doesn’t reduce rank distance — worse.\n\nSame with f8, g8 — retreating.\n\nSo best moves are those advancing: e6, f6, g6.\n\nOf these, **g6** is most direct toward the enemy.\n\nReason: Reducing both file and rank distance efficiently while aligning with the opponent\'s king for better control, even though checkmate is impossible. It follows king-centralization and approach principles.\n\nAnswer: K: f7-g6'}
